Matrix treatments of logic have a substantial history. Boolean matrix algebras were developed for
computer manipulation of logical functions well before CMs [Edwards, 1972]. Matrix logic and vector
logic treat logical connectives as operators, including outer-product operator bases and matrices
acting on Kronecker products of truth vectors [Stern, 1988, Mizraji, 1992, 2008]. Semi-tensor-product
work gives numeric logical structure matrices for Boolean functions and network dynamics [Cheng
and Qi, 2010, Zhao et al., n.d.], while Cheng et al. [2011] explicitly uses Boolean matrix operations
with XOR as addition and AND as multiplication. Accordingly, this paper does not claim that
truth tables, Boolean matrix products, logical matrix operators, or pointwise combination of truth
values are new.
The contribution sought here is narrower and computationally testable: a typed pipeline that
represents a binary connective as a compact CM, records its operand frame, aligns signed variable
pairs through explicit matrix transformations, fuses only exact compatible frames, and exposes
a fallback when structural fusion is unavailable. The mathematical identities are elementary or
antecedented; their role is to make the compiler transformation precise enough to prove and measure.
The paper makes the following contributions.
1. It defines CM evaluation as XOR–AND contraction over GF(2) and separates this algebra
from ordinary real or integer matrix multiplication.
2. It gives a typed operand-frame calculus for exchange, input polarity, output negation, and
sound same-operands fusion.
3. It separates and proves the structural alignment/fusion rule, exact four-assignment retabulation,
and the top-level admitted compiler path.
4. It defines formula-valued LMs and proves that valuation produces a CM in the corresponding
frame and commutes with logical pairing.
5. It separates LMs and CMs from external ANF and STP coordinate representations, preventing
several superficially similar objects from being treated as the same mathematical type.
6. It specifies an empirical boundary question: when do normalization and fusion reduce total
work after preparation, compilation, memory, kernel, and interface costs are all counted?
