3.1 Compiler rule and soundness boundary
Fix distinct declared variables R and C in disjoint row and column layout sets. A structural pair
result Pair(R, C, t) is admitted only for a signed binary primitive, the negation of an admitted pair,
or the fusion of two admitted children carrying identical (R, C) metadata. Its four-bit token satisfies
Et(r, c) = e[r/R, c/C] for all r, c ∈ B, (16)
after any fixed substitutions, which may not include R or C on a returned pair path. Expressions
with one live row variable and one live column variable may instead use an explicitly labeled four￾assignment retabulation. Repeated- variable expressions such as X Θ X, overlapping row/column
layouts, compound operands treated as opaque, and different variable pairs are outside the structural
rule.
Theorem 1 (Structural alignment-and-fusion soundness). If the structural rules return Pair(R, C, t)
for e, then (16) holds.
Proof. Use structural induction over an admitted construction. A signed primitive is correct by
(13); complementing a correct child represents negation; and fusing correct children with identical
metadata is sound by (15).
Lemma 1 (Exact pair retabulation). If the live support after fixed substitution is exactly one
distinct row variable R and one distinct column variable C, evaluating e at the four assignments
(1, 1),(1, 0),(0, 1),(0, 0) produces the unique token satisfying (16).
Theorem 2 (Soundness of the admitted pair compiler). Whether produced structurally or by exact
pair retabulation, every returned Pair(R, C, t) satisfies (16). If neither path is admitted, the ordinary
CM IR evaluates the unchanged expression.
