6 Experimental method
The confirmatory evaluation is designed around a conditional question: where, if anywhere, does
operand-aligned token compilation reduce user-visible cost? All arms consume the same immutable
AST and declared variable order. The structural-pair arm is compared with (i) a retabulation-only
ablation that derives the token by four scalar evaluations, (ii) the ordinary CM IR under the
same materialization policy, (iii) a direct sharing-aware packed evaluator of the source AST, (iv) a
lowered sharing-aware packed program for prepared reuse, (v) an ROBDD with declared variable
order [Bryant, 1986], and, for translatable circuit workloads, (vi) an external AIG flow. A four-bit
LUT query is also reported after both the packed evaluator and CM compiler have produced the
same truth token. NPN canonicalization is measured only where it is actually invoked by the
synthesis baseline; it is not charged to CM or presented as an evaluation algorithm. Backend-native
preprocessing is allowed but is timed and reported.
For the direct two-variable packed arm, assignment bit positions are (00, 01, 10, 11) from least
to most significant. The row and column masks are therefore 11002 and 10102, respectively. One
memoized traversal applies the source connectives as machine-bit operations and returns a four-bit
truth word; read most-significant bit first, that word is exactly the CM token order (11, 10, 01, 00).
This baseline does not construct a 2 × 2 array, perform four separate scalar traversals, or use CM
alignment rules. It is consequently the closest direct test of whether structural pair recognition
saves work over ordinary bit-parallel truth-table evaluation [Lee et al., 2022].
